\section*{Polynomial deterministic two-way simulation of one-way NFAs}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that one-way relational liveness has small nondeterministic recognizers but exponentially large deterministic two-way recognizers, uniformly over the growing finite alphabets.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For each $h\ge2$, use all binary relations on $[h]$ as letters and set
\[
 \operatorname{OWL}_h=\{R_1\cdots R_k:R_1\cdots R_k\ne\varnothing\}.
\]
Composition follows paths from left to right; the identity empty product makes the empty word accepted. The language is claimed recognizable by an automaton using nondeterministic transitions, $h+3$ states and only right or stay moves. By contrast, every deterministic two-way automaton recognizing it with $s$ states obeys
\[
 4(s+2)^2\ge2^{\lfloor(h-2)/31\rfloor}.
\]
The target may use stay moves, partial transitions and nonaccepting infinite runs. If an accepting initial configuration counts as success, the sharper $s+1$ replaces $s+2$. The alphabet is not fixed independently of $h$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25), deterministic computations are represented by local matching diagrams and a sink tour. Composition passes to relation-monoid corners, where lifted permutations and rank-loss amplification bound the size of any image that separates live and empty products.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: one-way relational liveness has small nondeterministic recognizers but exponentially large deterministic two-way recognizers, uniformly over the growing finite alphabets.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{OneWayLiveness.lean}, \texttt{TwoWayComplementation.lean}, \texttt{TwoWayDeterminization.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.OneWayLiveness.main\_theorem}, \texttt{OAI.TwoWayComplementation.complementation\_lower\_bound\_finite}, \texttt{OAI.TwoWayComplementation.explicit\_family\_main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

